{
  "abstract": "Orthogonalized optimizers such as Muon are known to remove activation outliers, which helps 4-bit models. We show the opposite holds for weight-only quantization, explain why, and test what recovers it. In 26M–86M-parameter decoders trained on 0.5B tokens, per-channel INT4 rounding of block weights costs Muon-trained models 2.6× more loss than an AdamW-trained model of equal quality, with no shrinkage across sizes, while activation-only INT4 costs Muon .72× as much. Relative weight rounding error is identical. What differs is how well weights align with their real inputs: a simple alignment factor is .62× as large under Muon and predicts the aggregate loss ratio (2.57× predicted, 2.64× measured). Weight outliers, dominant singular directions and energy in unexcited input directions are ruled out by preregistered tests. Partially de-orthogonalizing updates during the warmdown raises alignment and cuts the weight penalty dose-monotonically (−43%, −57%), so orthogonalized updates cause the misalignment, but the change costs too much bf16 quality to be a method. Late quantization-aware training recovers .545 ± .016 of Muon's penalty over three seeds; the AdamW baseline is run-to-run nondeterministic, which leaves its recovery unresolved. Input-aware rounding (GPTQ) helps Muon disproportionately, removing 56–65% of its penalty against 39–44% for AdamW, and at 26M matches late QAT without training. All tests were preregistered; we report four of our own predictions that failed.",
